    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Recent developments in the field of biologging have enabled researchers to study the fine-scale behaviors of large pelagic predators like the white shark (Carcharodon carcharias). I report on the use of a CATS camera tag to record high-definition video-image capture with a suite of biologging sensors, including a tri-axial accelerometer, to archive high frequency data on the fine-scale movements of the animal it is attached to. This study utilizes five deployments of CATS Cam tags on white sharks at two pinniped rookeries along the coast of California to analyze how the sharks budget their time and energy. My analyses indicate white sharks spend a large majority of their time (98.83% ± 0.6245) engaging in steady-state continuous swimming activity, which may be associated with a minimal cost transport. Differences in tail beat frequency (TBF) and overall dynamic body acceleration (ODBA) were observed throughout the deployments and indicate that most of the time these sharks travel at relatively consistent but low speeds and that high TBF and ODBA rates representing periods of increased activity are rare. Quantifying these fine-scale behaviors white sharks engage in while inhabiting coastal environments is essential to building a more complete understanding of their life histories and ecosystem dynamics and informing our conservation efforts.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
